                <h2>Opinion: Account Tiering Is Not Optional—It's Essential for Multi-Account Teams</h2>
<p>When advertising teams manage more than 10 accounts, efficiency often plummets: creative updates fall behind, budget allocation becomes chaotic, and policy violations lead to business disruptions. The root cause isn't lack of effort—it's the absence of a scientific account tiering management system.</p>
<p>The core objective of account tiering is <strong>directing the right resources to the right accounts</strong>, rather than maintaining all accounts with equal effort. Below is a practical tiering framework based on real operational experience.</p>
<h2>The Three-Tier Model for Account Management</h2>
<p>Based on advertising goals and risk profiles, accounts should be divided into three tiers:</p>
<h3>Tier 1: Core Accounts (Recommended: 20% of total, 50%+ of budget)</h3>
<p>These are the "cash cows" of the team, responsible for core business user acquisition. Characteristics include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Stable historical performance with ROAS consistently above team average</li>
<li>Large daily budgets, typically 3-5x other accounts</li>
<li>Mature product categories with predictable creative iteration cycles</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Management Strategy</strong>: Assign senior media buyers for key maintenance, monitor core metrics daily, and maintain a creative update frequency of 2-3 days per set. Reserve 10-15% budget flexibility to handle algorithm learning fluctuations.</p>
<h3>Tier 2: Testing Accounts (Recommended: 50% of total, 30% of budget)</h3>
<p>Testing accounts validate new creatives, targeting options, and products. While numerous with low individual budgets, they serve an irreplaceable risk-exploration function.</p>
<p><strong>Management Strategy</strong>: Implement standardized SOPs including testing cycles (typically 3-5 days), metric thresholds (CTR &gt; 1%, CPA &lt; 120% of target), and promotion rules (consecutive 3-day达标 can trigger creative migration to core accounts).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A utility app team once lacked proper testing account management, causing new creatives to be either abandoned prematurely or migrated too early to core accounts, resulting in budget waste. After implementing tiering management, their creative incubation cycle shortened from an average of 7 days to 4 days, improving testing efficiency by approximately 40%.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Tier 3: Risk Accounts (Recommended: 30% of total, 20% of budget)</h3>
<p>Includes newly opened accounts, previously violated accounts, and high-risk regional campaigns. These accounts feature <strong>coexisting potential gains and risks</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Management Strategy</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>New accounts require a 7-day observation period with budget ceiling locked at 30% of team average</li>
<li>Assign separate payment methods, financially isolated from core accounts</li>
<li>Accounts with violation history must complete platform appeal processes before reactivation</li>
</ol>
<h2>Three Key Actions for Team Collaboration</h2>
<p>The tiering model is merely a framework—implementation requires three key actions:</p>
<p><strong>1. Account Naming Standards</strong></p>
<p>Use a <code>Tier-Product Line-Region-Date</code> naming structure, for example: <code>L1-Tools-NA-2024Q2</code>. Standardized naming helps team members quickly locate accounts and facilitates financial reconciliation by tier.</p>
<p><strong>2. Weekly Account Health Review</strong></p>
<p>Schedule weekly account health checks at a fixed time (e.g., Monday morning), focusing on:<br />
- Whether core accounts show declining ROAS trends<br />
- Whether testing accounts have creatives meeting promotion criteria<br />
- Whether risk accounts have new violation alerts</p>
<p><strong>3. Dynamic Budget Allocation Mechanism</strong></p>
<p>Instead of fixed budget percentages, establish adjustment rules:<br />
- Testing accounts with single creatives achieving &gt;150% target ROAS for 3 consecutive days → eligible for 50% budget increase<br />
- Core accounts with daily ROAS dropping below threshold by 20% → automatic budget reduction with alert trigger</p>
<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<p><strong>Mistake 1: Equal Maintenance Frequency for All Accounts</strong></p>
<p>This causes core accounts to receive insufficient attention while testing accounts waste optimizers' energy. The essence of tiering management is differentiated resource allocation.</p>
<p><strong>Mistake 2: Fear of Adjusting Tier Assignments</strong></p>
<p>Account tiers are not permanent labels. If core accounts consistently underperform the testing account average for two consecutive weeks, a demotion review should be triggered. Similarly, well-performing testing accounts should be promptly upgraded.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The efficient operation of multi-account advertising teams depends not on how hard team members work, but on whether a scientific account tiering mechanism has been established. Get account classification right first, then pursue optimization efficiency.</p>
<p>If you're managing more than 5 advertising accounts, start with the three-tier model from this article. Prioritize completing account naming standards and weekly review mechanisms—these two changes typically yield noticeable improvements within 2 weeks.</p>
